// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// womb/kernel/arch/riscv64/bootinfo.vuma
// riscv64 bootinfo — populates the canonical superset BootInfo from SBI/DTB.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// This file is the riscv64 arch-side companion to womb/kernel/bootinfo.vuma
// (the canonical superset). It re-declares the superset BootInfo layout
// byte-identically (VUMA has no `import` — see kernel-developer-guide.md §9
// "Byte-identical re-declaration invariant"), then provides:
//
//   bootinfo_init(bi)
//       The same zero-all-fields helper as the canonical file. Re-declared
//       here so this file compiles standalone.
//
//   riscv_bootinfo_init(bi, hartid, dtb_addr, mem_start, mem_size)
//       riscv64-specific init: calls bootinfo_init first (clean slate),
//       then sets boot_type=2 (sbi — the RISC-V supervisor binary
//       interface), and records the device tree blob address plus the
//       usable-RAM window in mem_start/mem_size.
//
//       The DTB parser is stubbed for hosted mode: the caller passes the
//       dtb_addr (a0 at kernel entry, per the RISC-V boot protocol —
//       RISC-V boot protocol spec §3 / Documentation/riscv/boot.rst) and
//       the RAM window directly. K13+ will replace the stub with a real
//       flat device-tree walker (same FDT format as aarch64 — parse the
//       DTB magic 0xd00dfeed, walk /memory@n/reg, /chosen/bootargs,
//       /chosen/framebuffer, all PMT-pure typed-memory views).
//
//       Note on `hartid`: the RISC-V boot protocol leaves the hart ID
//       (hardware thread ID) in a0 at kernel entry when a DTB is present
//       in a1 — but Linux/riscv currently passes the DTB in a1 and hartid
//       in a0 (the older protocol put DTB in a1, hartid in a0; the newer
//       EFI-stub protocol puts the FDT in a1, hartid in a0 — same as
//       before). The current R1c stub accepts hartid as a parameter (for
//       signature compatibility with the boot stub that will call this
//       function) but does NOT store it in the superset BootInfo — there
//       is no hartid field in the canonical superset (the boot CPU's
//       hartid is not interesting to kmain; only secondary-CPU bringup in
//       Wave K6 cares, and that walks the DTB's /cpus node directly).
//       The hartid parameter is therefore intentionally unused in the
//       stub — same VUMA-calling-convention pattern as x86_bootinfo_init's
//       unused `mbi_addr` and sys_exec's `_entry`/`_stack` (commit
//       d25d68ca). K13+ may revisit if a use case emerges.
//
// ── RISC-V boot protocol recap (for K13+ implementer) ───────────────────────
//   At kernel entry on riscv64 (M-mode firmware jumps to S-mode kernel via
//   SBI), the CPU registers contain:
//     a0 = hartid   (the hart ID of the boot hart — u64).
//     a1 = dtb_addr (physical address of the device tree blob — u64).
//   The DTB is the same flat device-tree format as aarch64 (Devicetree
//   Spec v0.4 §5) — see womb/kernel/arch/aarch64/bootinfo.vuma for the
//   FDT header + token-format recap.
//
//   The K13+ real implementation:
//     1. Verify DTB magic at dtb_addr == 3735905741 (0xd00dfeed).
//     2. Walk the structure block — for each node:
//          /memory@n with property "reg" — read u64 base + u64 size (the
//            riscv default is #address-cells=2, #size-cells=2 on RV64),
//            pick the largest RAM region, set mem_start/mem_size.
//          /chosen with property "bootargs" — set bi.cmdline = prop_data_addr.
//          /chosen with property "linux,initrd-start"/"linux,initrd-end" —
//            record initramfs location (K13+).
//          /chosen/framebuffer (or /framebuffer) with properties "width",
//            "height", "reg" — set bi.framebuffer / fb_width / fb_height.
//          /cpus/cpu@n with property "reg" — list of hart IDs (for K6
//            secondary-CPU bringup — out of scope for R1c).
//     3. Set bi.boot_type = 2 (sbi).
//
//   For now (hosted mode / Wave R1c), the stub just stores the caller-
//   supplied dtb_addr / mem_start / mem_size unchanged and leaves
//   cmdline/framebuffer at 0. The stub is sufficient for kmain's hosted-
//   mode smoke tests (which only read boot_type/mem_start/mem_size/dtb_addr).
//
// ── PMT discipline ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   NO `*ptr`, `&x`, `allocate`, or `free` anywhere in this file. The
//   BootInfo is a flat typed-memory record; the caller allocates it via
//   `state_new(BootInfo)` and passes it by reference to
//   riscv_bootinfo_init (init-style API — same convention as the canonical
//   file).
//
//   In particular, the K13+ DTB parser will NOT use raw pointer arithmetic
//   to walk the DTB — it will construct `layout FdtHeader = { magic: u32,
//   totalsize: u32, ... }` and `layout FdtProp = { len: u32, nameoff: u32 }`
//   views over the dtb_addr region and use typed field access. (This is the
//   same PMT pattern K9a uses for sk_buff views: define a layout, cast the
//   State to it, read fields by name.)
//
// ── Decimal constants ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   All numeric literals in this file are decimal (per the project-wide
//   convention). Hex values are documented in comments only.
//     2149580800  = 0x80200000     (typical riscv64 kernel-entry physical
//                                    address — QEMU `-M virt` loads the
//                                    S-mode kernel at 0x80200000, after
//                                    the SBI firmware at 0x80000000; used
//                                    as the self-test's mem_start)
//     2181038080  = 0x82200000     (32 MB above 0x80200000 — used as the
//                                    self-test's dtb_addr; QEMU's `-M virt`
//                                    places the fw-cfg DTB somewhere in
//                                    this vicinity)
//     268435456   = 0x10000000     (256 MB — self-test mem_size)
//     3735905741  = 0xd00dfeed     (DTB magic — K13+ parser checks this)
//
// ── Build & verify (hosted x86_64) ──────────────────────────────────────────
//     . "$HOME/.cargo/env" && CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 \
//         cargo build --profile release-fast --bin compile_dump
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//         womb/kernel/arch/riscv64/bootinfo.vuma \
//         /tmp/riscv_bootinfo.bin x86_64 --verify
//     /tmp/riscv_bootinfo.bin ; echo "exit=$?"
// Expected: IVE Pass, exit=0 (riscv_bootinfo_init sets boot_type=2,
// dtb_addr=2181038080, mem_start=2149580800, mem_size=268435456; the 4
// self-test checks pass).
//
// Cross-arch sanity (the file should also IVE-verify on riscv64 itself —
// the superset layout is genuinely arch-independent):
//     ./target/release-fast/compile_dump \
//         womb/kernel/arch/riscv64/bootinfo.vuma \
//         /tmp/riscv_bootinfo_native.bin riscv64 --verify
// (Binary can't EXEC on this x86 host — exit 126 "Exec format error" — but
// the IVE verification step is what matters for arch-independence.)

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Layout — Canonical superset BootInfo (byte-identical re-declaration).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// MUST match womb/kernel/bootinfo.vuma byte-for-byte (same field order,
// same types, same names). Any drift would silently produce different
// field offsets at the call site — see kernel-developer-guide.md §9.
layout BootInfo = {
    mem_start: u64,
    mem_size: u64,
    cmdline: u64,
    dtb_addr: u64,
    multiboot_magic: u64,
    framebuffer: u64,
    fb_width: u32,
    fb_height: u32,
    boot_type: u8,
    _pad: [u8; 7],
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// bootinfo_init: zero every field of a caller-allocated BootInfo.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Same as the canonical womb/kernel/bootinfo.vuma::bootinfo_init —
// re-declared here so this file compiles standalone. riscv_bootinfo_init
// calls this first to get a clean slate before populating riscv64-specific
// fields.
fn bootinfo_init(bi: State<BootInfo>) {
    bi.mem_start = 0;
    bi.mem_size = 0;
    bi.cmdline = 0;
    bi.dtb_addr = 0;
    bi.multiboot_magic = 0;
    bi.framebuffer = 0;
    bi.fb_width = 0;
    bi.fb_height = 0;
    bi.boot_type = 0;
    let i = 0;
    while i < 7 {
        bi._pad[i] = 0;
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// riscv_bootinfo_init: populate the superset BootInfo from RISC-V boot regs.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   bi         caller-allocated State<BootInfo> (modified in place).
//   hartid     the boot hart ID (a0 at kernel entry per the RISC-V boot
//              protocol). NOT stored in the superset BootInfo — there is
//              no hartid field in the canonical layout (the boot CPU's
//              hartid is not interesting to kmain; only secondary-CPU
//              bringup in Wave K6 cares, and that walks the DTB's /cpus
//              node directly). Accepted as a parameter for signature
//              compatibility with the boot stub that will call this
//              function; intentionally unused in the stub. Per the VUMA
//              calling convention, unused parameters are silently dropped
//              (no warning, no error) — same pattern as x86_bootinfo_init's
//              unused `mbi_addr` and sys_exec's `_entry`/`_stack` in
//              womb/kernel/proc/exec.vuma (commit d25d68ca).
//   dtb_addr   physical address of the device tree blob (a1 at kernel
//              entry per the RISC-V boot protocol). Stored unchanged in
//              bi.dtb_addr so kmain can later pass it to the DTB parser
//              (K13+).
//   mem_start  start of usable RAM (physical byte address). On riscv64
//              QEMU `-M virt` the S-mode kernel is loaded at 0x80200000
//              (= 2149580800); the RAM window above the SBI firmware at
//              0x80000000 starts there.
//   mem_size   size of usable RAM in bytes. Stored unchanged in bi.mem_size.
//
// Behavior:
//   1. bootinfo_init(bi)    — zero everything (clean slate).
//   2. bi.boot_type = 2     — sbi (riscv64).
//   3. bi.dtb_addr = dtb_addr.
//   4. bi.mem_start = mem_start.
//   5. bi.mem_size  = mem_size.
//   6. (K13+) Parse DTB at dtb_addr → cmdline (from /chosen/bootargs),
//      framebuffer / fb_width / fb_height (from /chosen/framebuffer or
//      /framebuffer), refine mem_start/mem_size from /memory@n/reg.
//
// State<T> params: 1 (bi). The hartid / dtb_addr / mem_start / mem_size
// params are pure u64 values (no State indirection). The body is 4 field
// writes after the bootinfo_init call — pure typed-memory writes, no
// pointer ops.
fn riscv_bootinfo_init(bi: State<BootInfo>, hartid: u64, dtb_addr: u64, mem_start: u64, mem_size: u64) {
    bootinfo_init(bi);
    bi.boot_type = 2;            // sbi (riscv64)
    bi.dtb_addr = dtb_addr;
    bi.mem_start = mem_start;
    bi.mem_size = mem_size;
    // hartid is intentionally unused — see the doc comment above (the
    // canonical superset BootInfo has no hartid field; K6 secondary-CPU
    // bringup walks the DTB /cpus node directly). Recorded in the
    // signature for compatibility with the boot stub that calls this
    // function (the boot stub has a0=hartid, a1=dtb_addr in registers
    // and passes both to us). Same "unused param silently dropped"
    // pattern as x86_bootinfo_init's mbi_addr.
    //
    // Stub: the K13+ real implementation will walk the DTB at dtb_addr to
    // extract cmdline (from /chosen/bootargs), framebuffer info, and
    // refine mem_start/mem_size from /memory@n/reg. For now the caller
    // passes mem_start/mem_size directly — sufficient for hosted mode.
}

// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Self-test (main).
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Exercises the riscv_bootinfo_init API end-to-end:
//   1. Allocate a fresh BootInfo.
//   2. riscv_bootinfo_init(bi, 0, 2181038080, 2149580800, 268435456)
//        hartid     = 0           (boot hart)
//        dtb_addr   = 0x82200000 = 2181038080  (QEMU `-M virt` DTB area)
//        mem_start  = 0x80200000 = 2149580800  (QEMU `-M virt` kernel base)
//        mem_size   = 0x10000000 = 268435456   (256 MB)
//   3. Check boot_type  == 2          (sbi).
//   4. Check dtb_addr   == 2181038080 (0x82200000).
//   5. Check mem_start  == 2149580800 (0x80200000).
//   6. Check mem_size   == 268435456  (0x10000000).
//
// Returns 0 on success, 1..4 on the 4 individual check failures:
//   1: boot_type != 2
//   2: dtb_addr  != 2181038080
//   3: mem_start != 2149580800
//   4: mem_size  != 268435456
fn main() -> i32 {
    let bi = state_new(BootInfo);
    riscv_bootinfo_init(bi, 0, 2181038080, 2149580800, 268435456);
    if bi.boot_type != 2 { return 1; }
    if bi.dtb_addr != 2181038080 { return 2; }
    if bi.mem_start != 2149580800 { return 3; }
    if bi.mem_size != 268435456 { return 4; }
    return 0;
}
